WEBVTT

00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:01.434
but we're not doing therapy.

00:02:01.458 --> 00:02:03.893
EP: We may do therapeutic maneuvers.

00:02:03.917 --> 00:02:06.476
AG: OK, good. Because I feel
like I could use some.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.143
I'm excited to have this conversation
because every time I've heard you talk

00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:11.643
about romantic relationships,

00:02:11.667 --> 00:02:13.934
I find myself substituting in
work relationships

00:02:13.958 --> 00:02:16.268
and thinking that a lot
of the wisdom applies.

00:02:16.292 --> 00:02:18.434
I'm going to start
by asking you a question,

00:02:18.458 --> 00:02:21.726
which is, when you thought
about transitioning from,

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.351
kind of, the romantic world
to the work world,

00:02:24.375 --> 00:02:27.059
what did you see as
the big similarities and differences

00:02:27.083 --> 00:02:28.708
in how you look at relationships?

00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:31.101
EP: So it's interesting.